title: Mitnick / Novell 1994
page: breaches/mitnick-novell.html
as-of: 2026-11-22
promise: A Novell network administrator saw through fugitive Kevin Mitnick's pretext call and taped the first and only direct evidence naming him; Novell then watched him in a honeypot.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: 1 | cassette tape | the first and only direct evidence naming the hacker
stat: Live | every keystroke watched | through the honeypot terminal server
stat: 25 | counts in the federal indictment | naming Novell and five other companies
stat: 2.5 | years as a fugitive | targeting multiple companies
Shawn Nunley, the CSOH founder, was the Novell network administrator who set the voicemail trap in this chain.
notes:
Start with the tape, because the page does.

A Novell network administrator, Shawn Nunley, recorded a voicemail onto a cassette. The page calls it the first and only direct evidence naming the hacker, and the lynchpin of the federal case. Nunley is the CSOH founder, and the page says so. Say it too when you present.

Kevin Mitnick, known as "Condor", was already a fugitive and the FBI's most wanted hacker, living under a false identity in Denver. Novell was one target on a long list he worked for proprietary source code. The page gives his fugitive period as 1993 to 1995: 2.5 years targeting multiple companies.

After the tape, Novell gave him the access he had asked for, on a honeypot line where every keystroke was watched live through a terminal server.

The federal indictment ran to 25 counts, and named Motorola, Nokia, Fujitsu, Sun Microsystems and NEC as well as Novell. The page says they include copying proprietary Novell software, and that all he ever downloaded from Novell was inconsequential code, taken under surveillance. A later slide sets the two side by side.

== What happened
layout: timeline
event: Before he got anything | War dialing rings desk after desk across the San Jose campus, and Novell notices
event: Late one night | A caller posing as a real employee asks Nunley, at home, for modem access
event: The next morning | Nunley records the voicemail the caller left onto a cassette
event: With the tape in hand | Novell grants the access, on a honeypot line it watches
event: February 15, 1995 | Mitnick is arrested in Raleigh, after Tsutomu Shimomura helped track him there
Of these events, the page gives a full date only for the arrest. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
The page tells the story in order but gives a full date only for the arrest, and the timeline shows only that date.

War dialing came first. Phones rang desk after desk across the San Jose campus, and Novell's network team knew someone was seriously trying to get in before he had anything.

Late one night a caller using the name of Gabe Nault, a real Novell employee, phoned Nunley at home and asked for direct inbound modem access. Nunley asked him to leave a voicemail. It was waiting the next morning, and Nunley recorded it onto a cassette.

With the tape in hand, Novell granted the access, on a line built to identify him and watched keystroke by keystroke.

Mitnick stayed loose for some time after the tape, dialing into what the page calls Novell's fishbowl while the wider pursuit continued, until Tsutomu Shimomura helped track him to Raleigh, North Carolina. The page calls that chase its own famous story, and it does not tie the arrest to the honeypot. Mitnick was arrested on February 15, 1995.

The markers are evenly spaced, and the gaps between them are not to scale.

== The approach, and the call that gave him away
layout: flow
step: 01 | Reconnaissance | Names, teams and lingo | T1591
step: 02 | First Contact at Scale | War dialing tips Novell off | T1595
step: 03 | Pretexting and Elicitation | Pretext calls; a voicemail hijacked | T1598
step: 04 | The Turn | The request is the tell | T1566.004
Arrows show the page's stage order. Novell detected the war dialing before Mitnick got anything.
notes:
The page has nine stages, so the chain takes two slides. This one covers Mitnick's approach and the call that gave him away.

Stage 01 is research: employee names, team structures, internal project names and NetWare lingo. Stage 02 is war dialing the San Jose campus, which told Novell someone was trying to get in. Stage 03 is the pretext calls, which found who controlled the modems, and the hijacking of a real employee's voicemail. Stage 04 is the late-night call to that administrator at home, where the request itself was the tell.

Each box shows the stage's phase as the page labels it, and one ATT&CK technique the page maps to it.

Read the arrows as the page's stage order. The page says detection preceded access in this chain: the defenders heard the war dialing before Mitnick had anything.

== The tape, the honeypot and the arrest
layout: flow
step: 05 | Evidence Capture | The voicemail, taped to cassette | -
step: 06 | The Counter-Offensive | Access granted on a honeypot line | -
step: 07 | Full-Take Monitoring | Every keystroke watched live | -
step: 08 | Containment | Kept out of ATM, the source vault | -
step: 09 | Capture and Aftermath | Arrest in Raleigh | -
The page maps no ATT&CK technique to stages 05 to 09. It calls the chase to Raleigh its own famous story.
notes:
Stages 05 to 08 are Novell's; stage 09 is the arrest and what followed.

Stage 05 is the tape: the voicemail, recorded onto a cassette, the first and only direct evidence naming the hacker. Stage 06 is Novell granting the access he asked for, on a number that existed only to identify him. Stage 07 is the fishbowl: a terminal server where every keystroke was watched live and every session recorded. Stage 08 is containment: Mitnick learned of ATM, the system holding all NetWare source, and never reached it. Stage 09 is his arrest in Raleigh and what followed.

The page maps no ATT&CK technique to these five stages, so the boxes show none.

The arrows show order only. The page says Tsutomu Shimomura helped track Mitnick to Raleigh, and calls that chase its own famous story. It does not say the honeypot led to the arrest, so do not draw that line when you present.

== Mitnick studied Novell's names, teams and lingo first
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Reconnaissance
technique: T1591 | Gather Victim Org Information
technique: T1589 | Gather Victim Identity Information
fact: Sources | phone directories, manuals, product documentation, prior calls
fact: Learned | employee names, team structures, real project names, NetWare lingo
fact: Also on the list | Motorola, Nokia, Fujitsu, Sun Microsystems, NEC, per the indictment
The page calls this fluency a prerequisite for any convincing pretext.
notes:
Stage 01 is preparation. The page maps it to T1591, Gather Victim Org Information, and T1589, Gather Victim Identity Information.

Novell was one stop on a long target list. The federal indictment also names Motorola, Nokia, Fujitsu, Sun Microsystems and NEC, and the page says the tradecraft was the same everywhere.

Before making a single call, Mitnick invested significant time learning everything publicly available about his target. He gathered employee names from directory listings, understood Novell's internal team structures, learned real internal project names, and immersed himself in NetWare technical documentation so he could speak fluently about the product. NetWare was Novell's network operating system.

In The Art of Deception, Mitnick wrote that knowing the lingo and terminology establishes credibility. The page calls that fluency a prerequisite for any convincing pretext. His method included pretext calls: low-stakes calls to gather information for higher-stakes calls later.

OSINT, in the page's tags, stands for open source intelligence: information gathered from public sources.

No defense on the page is tied to this stage. That comes back in the discussion at the end.

== War dialing rang desk after desk, and Novell noticed
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: First Contact at Scale
technique: T1595 | Active Scanning
fact: Method | sequential calls across the San Jose campus phone ranges
fact: Listening for | a modem carrier that would answer
fact: The giveaway | phones ringing desk after desk: a scan you can hear
fact: Defender state | persistent threat confirmed, identity unknown
In this chain, the page says, detection precedes access.
notes:
Stage 02 is the war dialing. The page maps it to T1595, Active Scanning.

Already a fugitive, Mitnick opened the campaign the old-fashioned way: sweeping Novell's San Jose campus phone ranges number by number, hunting for a modem that would answer. A modem connects a computer to a telephone line. Desk phones rang sequentially through the building, one after another, for anyone paying attention to hear.

Novell's network team was paying attention. Before Mitnick ever got anything, the defenders knew somebody was seriously trying to get in. They did not know who. The page puts the defender state as persistent threat confirmed, identity unknown, and says the hunt for the hacker starts here.

In this chain, the page says, detection precedes access.

No defense on the page is tied to this stage either, and the second discussion question asks what you would add.

== Pretext calls found who controlled the modems
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Pretexting and Elicitation
technique: T1598 | Phishing for Information
technique: T1589 | Gather Victim Identity Information
fact: Posing as | an employee or developer, a different pretext per target
fact: Harvested | intelligence, not credentials
fact: Learned | Shawn Nunley controlled the modems; later, his home number
fact: Voicemail | Gabe Nault's, hijacked; a greeting planted in Mitnick's voice
The planted greeting pre-poisoned the verification channel for the pretext to come.
notes:
Stage 03 is the pretext calls. The page maps it to T1589, Gather Victim Identity Information, and T1598, Phishing for Information.

Mitnick worked Novell by phone, presenting himself as a legitimate employee or developer with a plausible reason for calling: real names, correct internal terminology, manufactured urgency, and a different pretext for each target. The page lists his levers as authority, urgency, likeability and reciprocity, and says he assessed each target's willingness to cooperate in the first few seconds and adapted in real time.

What the calls harvested was intelligence, not credentials. Somewhere in those conversations he learned who Shawn Nunley was, and that Nunley controlled the modems and all inbound access. Eventually he found Nunley's home phone number.

Around the same time he broke into the real Gabe Nault's voicemail and recorded a Vail out-of-office greeting in his own voice. The page calls that pre-poisoning the verification channel for the pretext to come, and it matters at stage 04.

The next slide explains phishing for information in general terms.

== What ATT&CK calls phishing for information
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1598 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1598/
credit: Also from ATT&CK T1598.004, Phishing for Information: Spearphishing Voice (attack.mitre.org)
ATT&CK says adversaries may phish, including by phone, to elicit sensitive information they can use during targeting. The objective is gathering data from the victim rather than executing malicious code.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK says adversaries may send phishing messages to elicit sensitive information that can be used during targeting. It separates this from Phishing, where the objective is executing malicious code: here the objective is gathering data from the victim.

Its voice sub-technique, Spearphishing Voice, covers phone calls used to elicit sensitive information, known as voice phishing or vishing. ATT&CK says such attempts frequently involve posing as a source with a reason to collect information, or creating a sense of urgency or alarm. It adds that adversaries may use information from earlier reconnaissance to tailor pretexts to be more persuasive and believable.

The page maps its stage 03 to T1598.

Also from ATT&CK T1598.004: https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1598/004/

== The late-night call: the request itself was the tell
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: The Turn
technique: T1566.004 | Phishing: Spearphishing Voice
technique: T1684.001 | Social Engineering: Impersonation
fact: Caller | "Gabe Nault", the name of a real Novell employee
fact: Story | the real Snowbird project; emergency code changes from Vail
fact: Backed by | Nault's voicemail greeting, which Mitnick had planted himself
fact: The tell | direct inbound modem access, against the dial-back policy
No genuine employee needed it. Nunley stalled: "please leave me a voicemail."
notes:
Stage 04 is where the chain turns. The page maps it to T1566.004, which ATT&CK names Spearphishing Voice, and T1684.001, Impersonation.

Late one night Shawn Nunley, the network administrator responsible for all of Novell's inbound connectivity, got a call at home from "Gabe Nault", a real Novell employee. The caller name-dropped Snowbird, a real internal top-secret project, and said he was on vacation in Vail and needed to make emergency code changes that could not wait. The story checked out, because the greeting on the real Gabe Nault's voicemail, the one Mitnick had planted there himself, backed it word for word.

What he wanted was direct inbound modem access. That request was the tell. Modem access at Novell ran through a secure dial-back system keyed to known phone numbers, plus manager approval, and Nunley owned that system.

Rather than confront him and burn the contact, Nunley played it cool. He could not grant the access from home anyway, so it would have to wait until morning, "but in case I forget, please leave me a voicemail."

== What checked out, and what gave him away
layout: compare
col: What checked out | A real employee's name, Gabe Nault | A real internal project, Snowbird | A vacation story his own planted greeting backed
col: What gave him away | The request: direct inbound modem access | It violated the dial-back policy and its approval workflow | He asked the administrator who owned that system
The page calls the pretext immaculate. The request itself was the tell.
notes:
This slide uses the page's own split.

The page calls the pretext immaculate: a real employee's name, a real internal project, and a vacation story he could "prove". Mitnick had hijacked the real employee's voicemail and planted the out-of-office greeting in his own voice, so call-back verification would confirm the impostor.

None of that changed what he was asking for. Direct inbound dial access violated the dial-back policy and its approval workflow, and the call reached the administrator who owned that system.

The page's counter: don't tip off the attacker. Stall politely, and convert the contact into a recorded artifact.

Two defenses later in the deck return to this slide: make call-back verification reach a live human rather than a mailbox, and pair training with clear policy, since the page says the trap worked because the defender knew the access policy cold.

== What ATT&CK calls impersonation and voice phishing
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1684.001 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1684/001/
credit: Also from ATT&CK T1566.004, Phishing: Spearphishing Voice (attack.mitre.org)
ATT&CK says adversaries may impersonate a trusted person, such as a colleague, to persuade a target to act for them. Its voice phishing technique covers calls aimed at system access. In this chain, the request gave the caller away.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK says adversaries may impersonate a trusted person or organization in order to persuade and trick a target into performing some action on their behalf. Its examples include impersonating an executive, a colleague or a third-party vendor. ATT&CK adds that impersonation is typically preceded by reconnaissance, such as gathering victim identity and organization information.

Spearphishing Voice, ATT&CK says, is a variant of spearphishing in which adversaries may use voice communications, such as a phone call, to ultimately gain access to victim systems. It frequently involves posing as a trusted source, or creating a sense of urgency or alarm.

The page maps its stage 04 to both techniques. In this chain, the page says, the request itself gave the caller away.

Also from ATT&CK T1566.004: https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566/004/

== The tape: the first and only direct evidence naming the hacker
layout: stage
stage: 05
phase: Evidence Capture
fact: Next morning | Nunley recorded the waiting voicemail onto a cassette
fact: Before the tape | no physical evidence tying Mitnick to a major crime
fact: What it proved | his identity, which no log or keystroke capture could establish
fact: In the case | primary evidence; Nunley the primary witness
The page calls it the lynchpin that turned suspicion into a prosecutable identity.
notes:
Stage 05 is the evidence. The page maps no ATT&CK technique to it.

The voicemail was waiting the next morning, and Nunley immediately recorded it onto a cassette for safekeeping.

Across years of intrusions at company after company, the page says, investigators had collected no physical evidence directly tying the crimes to Kevin Mitnick himself. A voice on tape, soliciting policy-breaking access to Novell's network, was the first and only direct evidence that the hacker was, in fact, Mitnick. It proved the one thing no log or keystroke capture could establish: the intruder's identity. As Nunley later put it, "You can't cross-examine a keystroke."

The recording became the primary evidence in the federal case, and Nunley the primary witness.

The page notes a fitting symmetry. Mitnick had weaponized a voicemail box to sell the pretext, and a voicemail box is what finally named him.

== Novell granted the access, on a number built to identify him
layout: stage
stage: 06
phase: The Counter-Offensive
fact: The lure | 1-800-37-TCPIP, provisioned solely for this one intruder
fact: The mechanism | toll-free billing shows the paying account the caller's number
fact: His evasion | found the DID number behind the 800 line and dialed it directly
fact: His side | dialing in at night from Denver, living as "Eric Weiss"
fact: Hiding his location | cloned cell phones, his standard practice
Mitnick spotted the caller-ID trap and dialed around it. He could not sidestep where the line landed.
notes:
Stage 06 is Novell's counter-offensive. The page maps no ATT&CK technique to it.

With the tape in hand, Novell gave "Gabe Nault" exactly what he had asked for. The dial-in came up on 1-800-37-TCPIP, a number provisioned for no purpose other than to see who called it. Toll-free billing delivers the caller's number to the account paying for the call, so every inbound call would unmask him. The page calls that number the ANI, short for automatic number identification.

Mitnick knew the phone system too well for that. He recognized what an 800 number would give away, dug through the phone system to find the DID number the 800 line translated to, and dialed that directly, sidestepping the caller-ID capture. DID stands for direct inward dialing: an ordinary number that rings through to a line.

On his side, he was dialing in at night from Denver, living as "Eric Weiss", with cloned cell phones his standard practice for hiding his location.

What he could not sidestep was where the line landed. That is the next stage.

== Every keystroke watched live, every session recorded
layout: stage
stage: 07
phase: Full-Take Monitoring
fact: Choke point | every session terminated on a Novell-controlled terminal server
fact: Live monitoring | real-time keystroke visibility for the network team
fact: Session recording | an early version of NutCracker, on the ethernet side
fact: Active control | sessions interrupted at will, especially near sensitive systems
Mitnick believed he was inside Novell. He was inside an instrumented box Novell had built for him.
notes:
Stage 07 is the fishbowl. The page maps no ATT&CK technique to it.

The DID trick bought Mitnick nothing that mattered, because the line terminated on a terminal server Novell controlled. A terminal server connects incoming lines to a network. The network team watched every keystroke of every session in real time, and an early version of NutCracker, hooked to the ethernet side of the terminal server, captured and recorded the sessions.

The page says the defenders also played with him, interrupting sessions when he got close to systems that mattered, or simply when they got bored.

The page calls him the most wanted hacker in America, and says he believed he was inside Novell when he was inside an instrumented box Novell had built for him. Its summary adds that he spotted the caller-ID trap and dialed around it, but never knew he was in a fishbowl.

The next slide draws where the line landed, as the page describes it.

== Where the honeypot line landed, as the page describes it
layout: layers
layer: Dial-in | 1-800-37-TCPIP, to capture his number | The DID behind it, which he dialed
layer: Terminal server | Novell-controlled: every keystroke watched live
layer: Ethernet side | An early NutCracker, recording the sessions
The 800 number translated to the DID, and either way the line landed on the terminal server.
notes:
This diagram shows the honeypot line from top to bottom. The arrows follow the page's description, from the dial-in to the terminal server and the recording behind it, nothing more.

The dial-in was 1-800-37-TCPIP, provisioned to capture the caller's number. Mitnick found the DID number the 800 line translated to and dialed it directly. Either way, the page says, the line terminated on a terminal server Novell controlled, where the network team watched every keystroke live.

An early version of NutCracker, hooked to the ethernet side of the terminal server, captured and recorded the sessions.

The page does not describe Novell's network beyond the terminal server, so the diagram stops there. What the operators did with that view, cutting sessions that got close to systems that mattered, is the next stage.

== Mitnick found ATM, the source vault, and never reached it
layout: stage
stage: 08
phase: Containment
fact: The prize | ATM, the internal repository holding all NetWare source code
fact: Seen live | what he was learning, as he learned it
fact: The block | sessions cut whenever he closed in on it
fact: Actual take | inconsequential source code, downloaded under full surveillance
Because the defenders could see him coming, he never reached it.
notes:
Stage 08 is containment. The page maps no ATT&CK technique to it.

Watching his sessions, the team could see what Mitnick was learning as he learned it. He discovered the existence of ATM, the internal system where Novell stored all of its source code. The page calls it the real prize. ATM is that system's name.

Because the defenders could see him coming, he never reached it: sessions that headed toward ATM got cut. Over the course of the operation he did manage to download some source code, but only inconsequential material, under full surveillance.

The page's summary puts it this way: Novell kept him off the ATM system that held all NetWare source, and all he ever downloaded was inconsequential code.

The next slide sets that beside what the federal indictment says.

== What the indictment says, and what the page adds
layout: compare
col: The federal indictment | 25 counts | Includes copying proprietary Novell software | Also names Motorola, Nokia, Fujitsu, Sun Microsystems and NEC
col: What the page adds | Everything he took from Novell, he took on camera | What he took from Novell was inconsequential, from the shallow end | He never reached ATM
The page says the Novell charge is true as far as it goes.
notes:
The page sets its account beside the legal record, and this slide keeps the two apart.

The federal indictment ran to 25 counts. The page says they include copying proprietary Novell software, and that the indictment also names Motorola, Nokia, Fujitsu, Sun Microsystems and NEC.

The page says the Novell charge is true as far as it goes. What the indictment does not say, according to the page, is that everything he took from Novell, he took on camera, out of the shallow end of the pool: inconsequential source code, downloaded while the network team watched. He never reached ATM.

When you present this, keep both halves. He did copy Novell software, and the page says what he took was inconsequential and taken under surveillance.

== Arrested in Raleigh; the Novell tape anchored the case
layout: stage
stage: 09
phase: Capture and Aftermath
fact: Arrest | February 15, 1995, in a Raleigh, North Carolina apartment
fact: The chase | Tsutomu Shimomura helped track him to Raleigh
fact: Found with | cloned phones, 100+ cloned phone codes, false identification
fact: Sentence | 46 months, plus 22 months for a supervised release violation
fact: Nunley | primary witness for five years, then challenged the DOJ over due process
When the FBI finally had him, the page says, the Novell tape anchored the case.
notes:
Stage 09 is the arrest and what followed. The page maps no ATT&CK technique to it.

Mitnick stayed loose for some time after the tape, until Tsutomu Shimomura helped track him to Raleigh, North Carolina, a chase the page calls its own famous story. He was arrested on February 15, 1995, with cloned cellular phones, more than 100 cloned phone codes and multiple pieces of false identification.

When the FBI finally had him, the Novell tape anchored the case. Nunley worked with the prosecution as its primary witness for five years. He grew increasingly troubled that Mitnick was held without a bail hearing and without access to the evidence against him. When a senior prosecutor told him on the record that the point was "sending a message to other would-be hackers", Nunley parted ways with the DOJ, the Department of Justice, and contacted Mitnick's defence team.

The sentence was 46 months, plus 22 months for a supervised release violation. Mitnick was released on a plea deal, and the man who trapped him became one of his closest friends for the rest of his life.

== The page's defenses against stages 01 to 04
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04
row: Spot the pretext; stall, capture, escalate | 04
row: Call-back that reaches a live human | 03 | 04
row: Training on the three pressure levers | 03
row: Training that pairs scenarios with policy | 04
row: Limits on support staff | -
row: Verify independently | -
No defense on the page is tied to stage 01 or 02.
notes:
This matrix and the next set the page's eight defenses against its nine stages, split where the two flow slides split. A cell is marked only where a defense's own words name something that stage has.

Spotting the pretext names stage 04: one admin recognized a policy-breaking request and stayed friendly. Call-back verification names stage 03's hijacked voicemail greeting, in Mitnick's own voice, and stage 04's access request by phone. Training on the three pressure levers names the levers stage 03 lists. Training that pairs scenarios with policy names stage 04's dial-back procedure.

Two defenses name no stage: restricting what support staff provide by phone, and the reminder that this attack still works today, so verify independently. That reminder mentions LinkedIn for OSINT as a modern tool, which is not an instruction, so column 01 stays empty.

No defense on the page is tied to stage 01, the research, or stage 02, the war dialing. That gap is the second discussion question.

== The page's defenses against stages 05 to 09
layout: matrix
columns: 05 | 06 | 07 | 08 | 09
row: Spot the pretext; stall, capture, escalate | 05
row: Grant access you fully instrument | 06 | 07 | 08
row: Monitor dial-up and remote access | 06
No defense on the page is tied to stage 09, the arrest and its aftermath.
notes:
Stages 05 to 08 are Novell's; stage 09 is the arrest and what followed.

Spotting the pretext names stage 05 too. The page says the only direct evidence that ever named Mitnick existed because the admin steered the attacker into leaving a recorded artifact.

Granting access you fully instrument names all three of the next stages: the number that existed only to identify him, stage 06; the terminal server that showed every keystroke live, with sessions recorded off the ethernet side, stage 07; and operators ready to cut any session that got close to something real, which is how stage 08 kept him out of ATM.

Monitoring dial-up and remote access names stage 06's detail: Mitnick connected at night from Denver.

No defense on the page is tied to stage 09, the arrest and its aftermath.

== Don't tip him off, then choose the terrain
layout: compare
col: Spot the pretext (04, 05) | Don't confront, don't comply | Stall politely and capture everything | Escalate to security while the pretext is still warm
col: Instrument the access (06 to 08) | Grant access you fully instrument | A number meant only to identify him; every keystroke shown live | Operators ready to cut sessions near anything real
The page calls honeypots, canary tokens and instrumented jump hosts the modern versions.
notes:
This slide gives two defenses from the page, each tied to the stages its heading names.

First, a defender who spots a pretext can end an entire campaign, if they don't tip off the attacker. The only direct evidence that ever named Mitnick existed because one admin recognized a policy-breaking request, stayed friendly, and steered the attacker into leaving a recorded artifact. Teach staff the play: don't confront, don't comply. Stall politely, capture everything, and escalate to security while the pretext is still warm.

Second, when you know the attacker is coming, choose the terrain: grant access you fully instrument. Novell gave "Gabe Nault" his dial-up on a number that existed only to identify him, into a terminal server that showed every keystroke live, with sessions recorded off the ethernet side and operators ready to cut any session that got close to something real. The attacker spent his effort where the defenders could see everything and he could reach nothing.

The page names honeypots, canary tokens and instrumented jump hosts as the modern versions of the same play.

== Verify by call-back, and know the policy cold
layout: compare
col: Call-back verification (03, 04) | Never give passwords, dial-up numbers or access to an inbound caller | Hang up; call back on a number verified from your internal directory | Make sure verification reaches a live human, not a mailbox
col: Training with clear policy (04) | The trap worked because the defender knew the access policy cold | Pair realistic scenarios with clear policy, not just documents | The page calls this the primary control against social engineering
Mitnick anticipated call-back checks and planted his "proof" in advance.
notes:
These two defenses answer stages 03 and 04.

First, implement a call-back verification procedure for any credential or access request by phone. Never provide passwords, dial-up numbers or system access to an inbound caller, however convincing they sound. Hang up and call back on a number you independently verify from your internal directory, and make sure verification reaches a live human, not a mailbox. Mitnick anticipated call-back checks and planted his "proof" in advance: a hijacked voicemail greeting, in his own voice, on the very number a verifier would dial.

Second, the page calls security awareness training the primary control against social engineering. The trap in this chain worked because the defender knew the access policy cold: the moment a request violated the dial-back procedure, the pretext collapsed, however good the backstory was. Regular training that pairs realistic scenarios with clear policy, not just policy documents, is what the page says separates a staff member who pauses and verifies from one who helps an attacker.

== Name the pressure levers; limit what goes out by phone
layout: compare
col: The three levers (03) | Authority, urgency and likeability | Mitnick used all three in every call, the page says | Very charming, knowledgeable and urgent at once: a red flag
col: Support staff limits (no stage) | No credentials, dial-up numbers or access details by phone without verification | A written procedure for the support desk | Authority to refuse without penalty
In this chain, the call went to the admin who owned inbound access.
notes:
These two defenses are both written for support staff in general.

First, train support staff to recognize the three pressure levers: authority, urgency and likeability. The page says Mitnick used all three in every call. When someone is very charming, very knowledgeable and very urgent all at once, that combination itself is a red flag. Slow down, verify, and never let urgency override procedure. The levers are the ones stage 03 lists.

Second, restrict what information support staff can provide, and to whom. Credentials, dial-up numbers and system access details should never be distributed by phone without a formal verification workflow. The support desk should have a written procedure and the authority to refuse without penalty. The page ties this defense to no stage.

Keep the chain itself straight when you present these. In this chain the call went to Shawn Nunley, the network administrator responsible for all of Novell's inbound connectivity, and he recognized the request.

== Watch remote access, and verify independently
layout: compare
col: Monitor dial-up (06) | Mitnick connected at night from Denver | Watch for unusual hours, unknown caller ID, high data volume | Anomalous remote access should trigger a review
col: Still works today (no stage) | The tools changed: AI voice cloning, LinkedIn for OSINT, SMS | The psychology is identical to what Mitnick did in 1994 | So is the defence: verify independently, never let urgency override process
notes:
These are the remaining two of the page's defenses.

First, monitor dial-up and remote access connections for unusual times or locations. Mitnick connected at night from Denver, the detail stage 06 records. Anomalous remote access, such as unusual hours, unknown caller ID or a high volume of data transferred, should trigger a review.

Second, the page says this attack still works today. Vishing, voice phishing, is social engineering by telephone. The tools have changed: attackers now use AI voice cloning, LinkedIn for OSINT, and SMS as a follow-up. The psychology is identical to what Mitnick did in 1994, and the page says the defence is identical too: verify independently, and never let urgency override process. It ties this defense to no stage.

Set that beside this chain. The pretext's details checked out, and a call-back would have confirmed them, because Mitnick had planted the voicemail greeting himself. It collapsed on the request: direct inbound access that broke the dial-back policy, made to the administrator who knew that policy cold.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Would a policy-breaking request by phone stand out to whoever owns that access?
check: Do staff know the play: don't confront, don't comply, stall, capture, escalate?
check: Does your call-back verification reach a live human, not a mailbox?
check: Do staff treat charm, knowledge and urgency at once as a red flag?
check: Can your support desk refuse a phone request without penalty?
check: Would remote access at unusual hours or from unknown callers trigger a review?
check: If you knew an attacker was coming, could you grant access you fully instrument?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask whether the person who owns a kind of access knows its policy well enough that a request breaking it stands out, the way it did at stage 04. Ask whether staff know the play for a suspected pretext: don't confront, don't comply, stall politely, capture everything, and escalate while the pretext is still warm.

Ask whether call-back verification reaches a live human rather than a mailbox, since Mitnick planted his proof on the very number a verifier would dial.

Ask whether staff treat charm, knowledge and urgency arriving together as a red flag, and whether the support desk has a written procedure and the authority to refuse without penalty.

Finally, ask whether anomalous remote access would trigger a review, and whether, if you knew an attacker was coming, you could give access you fully instrument.

== What could give the caller away?
layout: question
The caller used a real employee's name and a real secret project. A call-back would have confirmed his story, because he had planted the voicemail greeting himself.

What, then, could give him away?
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one.

The page calls the pretext immaculate. The caller used the name of Gabe Nault, a real Novell employee, and name-dropped Snowbird, a real internal top-secret project. His vacation story checked out, because Mitnick had hijacked the real employee's voicemail and planted the out-of-office greeting in his own voice.

Ask each person to name what, if anything, would have made them suspicious, before you show the answer slide. The answer slide gives what the page says the tell was.

== The page says the request itself was the tell
layout: answer
Direct inbound modem access broke the dial-back policy and its approval workflow. No genuine employee needed it, and the defender knew the policy cold.
- Don't confront, don't comply: stall politely and capture everything.
notes:
The page's answer is the request, not the caller.

Modem access at Novell ran through a secure dial-back system keyed to known phone numbers, plus manager approval, and Nunley owned that system. No genuine employee needed what this caller was asking for. The page's defenses put it this way: the moment a request violated the dial-back procedure, the pretext collapsed, no matter how good the backstory was.

Then comes what to do with that recognition. Rather than confront the caller and burn the contact, Nunley stalled politely and asked him to leave a voicemail, which became the tape.

Bring the group's ideas back to this. An idea that depends on spotting a flaw in the story runs against what the page says: the story checked out, and a call-back would have confirmed it.

== What would you add for stages 01 and 02?
layout: question
The page ties no defense to Mitnick's research or to the war dialing, though Novell heard the dialing before he got anything.

What would you add, and which reported detail would it catch?
notes:
This question comes from the gaps in the defense matrix.

The page offers defenses tied to stages 03 to 08, and two more that name no stage. It ties nothing to stage 01, where Mitnick learned names, teams, project names and NetWare lingo, or to stage 02, the war dialing that rang phones across the San Jose campus.

Stage 02 is also where the page says detection preceded access: the network team knew somebody was seriously trying to get in before Mitnick had anything.

Let the group propose ideas before you show the answer slide. Ask each proposal to name the detail from the page that it would have caught.

== The page ties no defense to stages 01 and 02
layout: answer
Any control you propose is your own suggestion. Test it against reported details:
- Stage 01: directory listings, real project names, NetWare lingo.
- Stage 02: desk phones ringing in sequence, a scan you can hear.
notes:
Be clear that the page stops here. It ties no defense to stage 01 or stage 02, and this deck adds none of its own. The page's reminder that this attack still works today mentions LinkedIn for OSINT, but as a modern tool, not as an instruction.

If the group proposes ideas, treat them as the group's own, and test each one against the facts the page reports.

For stage 01, those facts are the sources Mitnick used: phone directories, technical manuals, product documentation and prior calls to gather names, and the goal of enough authentic detail to withstand scrutiny from a real Novell employee.

For stage 02, they are the automated sequential calls across the campus phone ranges, listening for modem carriers, and the giveaway the page describes: phones ringing desk after desk in sequence. Novell's team noticed it, and the page says the hunt for the hacker started there.

